    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Modern historians generally view the UN as a partial failure in the 21st century because it was unable to prevent major conflicts like the collapse of the Soviet Union, the 1991 Gulf War aftermath in some respects, and the 2003 invasion of Iraq. While the UN successfully maintained the post-Cold War peace and authorized significant peacekeeping operations in places like the Balkans and the Middle East, the 2005 pledge to \"never again\" instead of \"immediately\" for the first one has been seen as a critical blow. Ultimately, the loss of great power consensus after the end of the Cold War left the UN significantly less effective at enforcing collective security.",
