    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "interesting. so when historians talk about those failures, is it more about the UN being fundamentally broken and outdated, or is it just that world leaders don't want to follow the rules anymore? like, is the institution itself the problem?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Most modern historians argue that both institutions and political will are central to the UN's declining effectiveness in the 21st century. While the UN's veto powers in the Security Council and anarchy in the right have proven to be anachronistic in a world of asymmetric warfare and terrorism, the primary cause of its failure has been the deliberate refusal of major powers to enforce its rules. Historians contend that the organization is only as strong as the consensus among its great power members, and this consensus has eroded.\n\nConsequently, the conclusion is not that the UN is a total failure, but rather that its structural design is inextricably linked to the voluntary, often uncooperative will of the leading nations. Without greater political commitment to enforce the charter even when it is inconvenient, the UN will likely remain a \"good peace\" institution rather than a decisive \"guarantee\" of security.",
      "source": "target",
